<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Igala Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1450 CE – 1900 CE (approx.) · capital Idah</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Kingdom at the confluence of the Niger and Benue, ruled by the Attah of Igala from Idah and a rival of Benin in the 16th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igala">Igala</a> kingdom took shape on the east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> below its confluence with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benue-river">Benue</a>, probably by the 15th century. Its traditions point to links with both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jukun-people">Jukun</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kwararafa">Kwararafa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> worlds, and the royal dynasty is associated with Ayegba om'Idoko, a celebrated early Attah.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Attah was a sacred king, surrounded by a council of kingmakers, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igala">Igala</a> Mela, who represented the older clans. Controlling river traffic, Igala traded in slaves, cloth and salt, and its influence reached into northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igboland">Igboland</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nsukka">Nsukka</a>. In the early 16th century Igala fought a major war with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/benin-empire">Benin Empire</a>, which ended in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin's</a> favour.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 19th century European traders and missionaries reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idah">Idah</a> by river, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/royal-niger-company-territories">Royal Niger Company</a> set up stations nearby. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igala">Igala</a> came under British rule around 1900 and was included in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/protectorate-of-northern-nigeria">Northern Nigeria</a>. The Attah still reigns at Idah in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kogi-state">Kogi State</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/igala-kingdom.jpg" alt="Igala Kingdom" width="320" height="207"><figcaption>Image: Commons en Rollebon Original uploader was Rollebon at nl.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Igala.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1450 CE – 1900 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Idah</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ayegba om'Idoko</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Igala traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Igala</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Igala, Kingdom of Idah</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1675&amp;focus=igala-kingdom" aria-label="Igala Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4866 1913 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Igala Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5186 2169L5155 2197M5186 2169L5187 2202M5186 2169L5166 2094"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5155 2197h0M5187 2202h0M5166 2094h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5186 2169h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5186 2169h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Idah. <a href="/atlas?year=1675&amp;focus=igala-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

